# Subagent Output Requirements

Delegated subagents return structured result blocks so the dispatching agent can act on results without re-reading transcripts. Each agent's own definition carries the full format; this rule is the contract summary.

## Implementer

`<implementer-result>` — status (COMPLETE | BLOCKED), files modified/created, tests written, and a self-review table (requirements implemented, no scope creep, stayed inside file ownership, tests passing). An honest `fail` row beats a clean-looking table.

## Spec Reviewer

`<reviewer-result>` — verdict (COMPLIANT | NON-COMPLIANT), the requirements checklist with file:line evidence, and every finding ranked by severity with confidence and a concrete fix. Coverage over filtering: findings are reported, not suppressed; the caller filters.

## Skill Author

`<skill-author-result>` — status, skill path, validation result (validate-skill.sh), and baseline-test outcome.

## Shared rules

- A blocked or aborted run still returns its block, with a one-line reason — silence is never a valid result.
- Blocks report evidence (paths, line numbers, command output), not adjectives.
- Dispatch prompts must be self-contained: subagents have no access to the parent conversation.
